  "final_text": "Both the dashboard and the alert rule now exist on the local Kibana 9.5.5.\n\n**Dashboard: `EFF-87da1741 Web logs overview`**\n- URL: http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-87da1741-web-logs-overview-b9bb737f9318\n- Panels: Total requests (count), Unique client IPs, 5xx responses (filtered to `response.keyword: 503`), Requests over time (area by `timestamp`), and Responses by status code (pie).\n- Time range is absolute, 2026-09-01 to 2026-12-31, because the sample data is dated 2026-09-27 to 2026-11-26. The default last-7-days range would show nothing.\n\n**Alert rule: `EFF-87da1741 5xx spike`** (id `09107e50-9148-4834-9b91-d1b9bde81e33`)\n- Type `.es-query` on `kibana_sample_data_logs`, time field `timestamp`.\n- Query matches `response.keyword` starting with `5`.\n- Runs every 1m, fires when the count over the last 5m is greater than 10.\n- I enabled it so it actually runs. Creation defaults to disabled.\n\n**Data notes**\n- 14,074 requests from 1,001 client IPs. Status codes are 200 (12,832), 404 (801), and 503 (441).\n- 503 is the only 5xx code in the data, so the \"5xx\" panel and rule currently cover only 503.\n- Top countries by request count are CN, IN, US, ID, and BR.\n\n**Things to know**\n- The rule has no connector attached, so when it fires nothing gets notified. It would need an action such as a Slack or server-log connector to send anything.\n- The rule's status was still \"pending\" right after creation. I did not confirm that it has run a check yet.\n- The sample timestamps run past today's date (2026-10-10), so the rule's 5-minute window may stay empty for a while. That is expected for this data, not a fault in the rule.\n\nI could not query Elasticsearch directly through the Kibana API. The internal ES|QL endpoint worked for profiling, so I used that.",
